---
import DocShell from '../../layouts/doc-shell.astro';
import { docsLinks } from '../../lib/nav';

const toc = [
   { id: 'install', label: 'Install' },
   { id: 'use', label: 'Use it' },
   { id: 'rules', label: 'What the skill teaches' },
   { id: 'mcp', label: 'Skill or MCP server' },
   { id: 'update', label: 'Update' },
];
---

<DocShell
   title="Install the agent skills"
   summary="<p>A skill is a set of instructions a coding agent loads when a task calls for it. The a11ied skills teach an agent how to test accessibility with a11ied: what to run first, which screen reader to use, and how to report what it found.</p>"
   links={docsLinks}
   toc={toc}
>
   <section>
      <h2 id="install">Install</h2>
      <pre><code>npx -y @a1st/aix install https://github.com/hypothesi/a11ied/blob/master/packages/skills/ai.json --only skills --user</code></pre>
      <p>
         <a href="http://aix.a1st.dev/">aix</a> finds the coding agents on your machine and
         asks which one gets the skills. <code>--user</code> installs them for every project.
         Add <code>--target claude-code</code>, <code>--target codex</code>, or another agent
         name to skip the question.
      </p>
      <p>This installs two skills.</p>
      <dl class="defs">
         <dt><code>a11ied</code></dt>
         <dd>For checking a component or page while you build it.</dd>
         <dt><code>a11ied-audit</code></dt>
         <dd>For a full WCAG audit with parallel workers and a report. See <a href="/guides/audit">audit a site with AI agents</a>.</dd>
      </dl>
   </section>

   <section>
      <h2 id="use">Use it</h2>
      <ol>
         <li>Install the CLI with <code>npm install --global a11ied</code>, or <a href="/guides/agents">connect the MCP server</a>. The skills work with either.</li>
         <li>Restart the agent so it loads the skills.</li>
         <li>Start the app you want to test.</li>
         <li>Ask for a test, and name the page.</li>
      </ol>
      <pre data-label="AI AGENT PROMPT"><code>Use the a11ied skill to test http://localhost:3000/checkout. Run the scan first. Then test the field labels and the error messages with the screen reader. Report the two separately.</code></pre>
   </section>

   <section>
      <h2 id="rules">What the skill teaches</h2>
      <dl class="defs defs--wide">
         <dt>Look up the criterion first</dt>
         <dd>When a task names a WCAG criterion, the agent reads how to test it before it tests anything.</dd>
         <dt>Match the test to the criterion</dt>
         <dd>A scan covers part of some criteria. The rest need a screen reader, the keyboard, or judgment.</dd>
         <dt>Start simulated, finish real</dt>
         <dd>The simulated screen reader for quick runs. VoiceOver or NVDA when the question is what the real one does.</dd>
         <dt>Keep results apart</dt>
         <dd>Scan results, transcripts, and judgments are reported separately. A clean scan is never reported as meeting WCAG.</dd>
         <dt>Label simulated output</dt>
         <dd>Output from the simulated screen reader is never presented as VoiceOver or NVDA.</dd>
      </dl>
   </section>

   <section>
      <h2 id="mcp">Skill or MCP server</h2>
      <p>
         The skills tell the agent how to test. The agent then needs a way to run a11ied,
         and there are two. With the CLI installed, it runs <code>a1</code> commands in a
         terminal. With the <a href="/guides/agents">MCP server</a> connected, it calls the
         matching tools. Both skills cover both, so install whichever suits your agent, or
         both.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section>
      <h2 id="update">Update</h2>
      <p>Run the install command again. It replaces the skills with the current ones.</p>
   </section>
</DocShell>
